<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kirrha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cirrha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kirrha was an ancient town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a> below Delphi, serving as the port for pilgrims to the oracle. Accused of mistreating and taxing visitors, it was destroyed by the Amphictyony in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sacred-war">First Sacred War</a> in the early 6th century BCE, and its plain was dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and left uncultivated. A later settlement on the site served Delphi as its harbour.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kirrha.jpg" alt="Kirrha" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Schuppi · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kirra-Shipyards.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The town stood in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> at the head of the Crissaean Gulf, at the mouth of the river Pleistus and the foot of Mount Cirphis. It grew up as the port of Crissa, an older town on a spur of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-parnassus">Mount Parnassus</a> a little south-west of Delphi, which Pausanias wrongly took to be the same place. Between the two lay a fertile plain: the small Cirrhaean plain on the coast and the larger Crissaean plain stretching to Crissa and Amphissa. The town drew its water by a canal from the Pleistus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>As the port grew, Crissa declined, and the sanctuary of Pytho grew into the town of Delphi, which claimed independence. The port commanded the approach to the temple by sea, levied heavy tolls on pilgrims and was said to have maltreated Phocian women returning from the temple.</p><p>The Amphictyons declared war on it about 595 BCE and took it after ten years, by a stratagem some ascribe to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a>. According to the story, the canal was turned off, filled with hellebore and let flow again, and the defenders were too weakened to hold their walls; a salt spring with a purgative effect rises near the ruins. The town was razed and curses laid on anyone who tilled or lived on its plain, whose bounds were inscribed on a wall of the temple at Delphi.</p><p>The spoils of the town paid for the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythian-games">Pythian Games</a>, whose hippodrome lay in the plain. The plain stayed untilled until the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>, when the Amphissians cultivated it and tried to rebuild the town; in the war that followed Philip took Amphissa in 338 BCE. The town was later rebuilt as the port of Delphi, and by the 2nd century it had a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leto">Leto</a> with statues of Attic work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site is a hill called Magoula Xeropigadas near the modern village of Kirra, which keeps the ancient name, in the municipal unit of Itea. In the mid-19th century walls enclosing a space about a mile in circuit could still be traced, with the foundations of many buildings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cirrha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the First Sacred War, traditionally dated about 595–586 BCE, the Amphictyons, aided by Cleisthenes of Sicyon and the Thessalians, destroyed <b>Kirrha</b>, accused of exploiting pilgrims, and consecrated its plain to the god, although many details of the story are doubted by modern historians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its role became prominent in the First Sacred War, traditionally c. 595-585 BCE, when members destroyed the town of <b>Kirrha</b> for abusing pilgrims and then reorganised the Pythian Games in 582 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/amphictyonic-league">Amphictyonic League</a></li></ul></section>
